  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on measurements from a single, clear-sky, low-wind summer day in a single temperate-climate city, which may limit their applicability to other weather conditions or climates.",
      "what": "Using mobile meteorological carts, this study measured air temperature, relative humidity, wind speed, and six-directional radiation across 29 diverse urban locations in Novi Sad, Serbia, during a hot, cloud-free summer day. The measurements were used to calculate Mean Radiant Temperature (MRT) and Physiological Equivalent Temperature (PET), which ranged from 25°C to 56°C. The analysis revealed that a 10% reduction in Sky View Factor (SVF) lowered MRT by up to 5°C, while surface type (grass versus impervious) had only minor effects on radiant load.",
      "why": "It isolates the specific directional radiative pathways that drive pedestrian heat stress, demonstrating that lateral shortwave radiation from local shading variability is the primary source of spatial PET differences, whereas surface materials play a negligible role compared to overhead geometry."
    }
  },
